203 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 116 0 R/T(1.1 The Problem)/E(1.1 The Problem)/Pg 12 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

204 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 116 0 R/E(Underground coal mining causes gradual surface subsidence that threatens nearby communities, infrastructure, agricultural land, and forests. Indian subsidence monitoring today relies mostly on manual field surveys and after-the-fact damage assessment, which cannot give timely warning before ground failure occurs. By the time visible damage appears, the underlying deformation has already occurred. The goal is an indigenous, low-cost, real-time, AI-enabled early warning system using a wireless surface mesh sensor network deployed above underground mine panels.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

205 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 116 0 R/T(1.2 Why This Is Genuinely Hard)/E(1.2 Why This Is Genuinely Hard)/Pg 12 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

206 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 962 0 R/E(Subsidence develops gradually \204 there is no obvious single moment where "normal" becomes "critical.")/Pg 12 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

207 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 963 0 R/E(Ordinary mining activity looks like early subsidence. Blasting, heavy machinery, and transport cause ground vibration and minor tilt that resembles early subsidence signals but isn't dangerous \204 false alarms erode trust in the system.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

208 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 964 0 R/E(Real subsidence failure events are rare, so there is very little labelled data to train a conventional ML model on.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

209 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 965 0 R/E(Node placement matters as much as the sensors themselves \204 sensors in the wrong place simply won't see the signal in time.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

210 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 966 0 R/E(A single sensing modality cannot distinguish mechanism. Tilt alone can't tell you why the ground is changing, which matters for both accuracy and explainability to a mining engineer or DGMS reviewer.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

211 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 116 0 R/T(1.3 Positioning Against Existing Solutions)/E(1.3 Positioning Against Existing Solutions)/Pg 12 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

