429 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(India uses multiple underground mining configurations. Longwall mining systematically extracts a large section of coal; the roof is allowed to cave behind the advancing face, producing a relatively broad, progressive pattern of ground movement \204 a Knothe-based physics/empirical model can give an expected deformation pattern here \("at this location, the model expects approximately X mm of subsidence at this stage"\), a physical quantity directly comparable to observations.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

430 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(Bord-and-Pillar mining works differently \204 coal is partially extracted while pillars are left to support the roof. Depending on mining stage, geology, depth, extraction geometry, and pillar condition, instability can develop very differently \204 particularly during depillaring, when pillars are progressively removed. The resulting failure mechanism can be substantially more localized \(pothole-style\) than the broad subsidence trough of longwall mining. Using the same physical model for both would be a poor assumption.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

431 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 134 0 R/T(PART 6 \204 Dual-Mode Mining Physics)/E(PART 6 \204 Dual-Mode Mining Physics)/Pg 30 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

432 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(Bord-and-Pillar represents over 90-95% of Indian underground coal production \(85.76% mechanised + 0.10% conventional\), vs. under 8% longwall \(7.63% mechanised + 0.74% conventional\) \204 most comparable proposals implicitly target the minority case. This is the single most defensible reason the dual-mode design matters \204 state it prominently.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[5]>>
