495 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 141 0 R/E(What it is: an established method from landslide early-warning science for converting an accelerating deformation trend into an estimated time-to-failure.)/Pg 37 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

496 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 141 0 R/E(How it works: as ground approaches structural failure, velocity doesn't increase linearly \204 it accelerates such that the inverse of velocity \(1/velocity\) decreases in an approximately straight line toward zero as failure approaches. At each time step, compute predicted deformation velocity, take its inverse, plot over recent time steps, fit a straight line, extrapolate forward to find where it crosses zero \204 that crossing point is the estimated time of failure \(inverse velocity of zero = theoretically infinite velocity = sudden collapse\).)/Pg 37 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

497 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 141 0 R/E(Uncertainty/confidence: the inverse-velocity extrapolation is run several times using slightly different variations of the model's output \(an ensemble \204 e.g. by slightly perturbing the recent input window\). The spread of resulting time-to-failure estimates converts into a confidence range \(e.g. "12-18 days, 75% confidence"\) instead of one falsely-precise number. time_to_threshold is always shown as a range, never a single date.)/Pg 37 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

